<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android has blocked plain http:// since API 28. Without this the app fails
  every request to a laptop on the LAN and simply looks broken, because there
  is no useful error surfaced to the webview.

  Why this allows cleartext everywhere:
  the obvious tighter version, listing private ranges like 192.168.0.0/16,
  does not work. A <domain> entry matches one exact hostname or IP, not a CIDR
  block, so "192.168.0.0" would match that single literal address and nothing
  else. The only ways to narrow it are to hardcode the exact laptop IP, which
  changes with every network and every DHCP lease, or to allow cleartext
  generally.

  This is a debug and testing build, so cleartext is on. It stops mattering
  once the backend moves to a hosted HTTPS URL: at that point delete this file
  and the two attributes in AndroidManifest.xml that reference it, and the app
  is HTTPS only again.

  Note for future edits: XML comments cannot contain a double hyphen anywhere,
  which is exactly what broke the first version of this file.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
